chapters=[
chapter('contract','01','A sorted list can still<br>be the wrong answer.','DEFINE SUCCESS',[
('First, choose the rule.',f'A <strong class="kw">key</strong> is the property you order by: price, date, name or size. These numbers use smallest first; “best” would need a definition. {cite("contract")}'),
('Order is only half the job.',f'Deleting the inconvenient numbers makes this list look ordered. But a true sort must keep <strong class="kw">every original item</strong>, including repeats. {cite("contract")}'),
('Make one small decision.',f'A computer can <strong class="kw">compare</strong> two keys, then exchange their records. A method must say which pair to inspect next, without relying on a person seeing the whole answer. {cite("elementary")}'),
('Keep something true.',f'In insertion sort, the processed prefix remains ordered after each insertion. This promise is an <strong class="kw">invariant</strong>: a fact that connects one step to the next. {cite("elementary")}'),
('Know why it stops.',f'The processed prefix gains an item each round, and the input is finite. A rule plus an invariant plus <strong class="kw">progress</strong> gives a reason to trust the result; now ask what that progress costs. {cite("elementary")}')]),
chapter('cost','02','The same result.<br>Very different amounts of work.','COUNT THE WORK',[
('Almost done is useful information.',f'An ordered input needs only a short confirmation in insertion sort. <strong class="kw">Adaptive</strong> algorithms can benefit from structure that is already present. {cite("elementary")}'),
('Reverse the input. Change the bill.',f'If every new item belongs at the front, an adjacent-swap insertion sort moves it past everything before it. This example needs 1 + 2 + … + 7 = <strong class="kw">28 swaps</strong>. {cite("elementary")}'),
('Break a big task into small ones.',f'<strong class="kw">Merge sort</strong> splits the sequence until the pieces are trivial to sort. The teaching example has eight items, so halving reaches singletons in three levels. {cite("merge")}'),
('Combine answers without starting over.',f'Merge two ordered runs by comparing their front values and taking the smaller. Each level processes the items again, giving growth proportional to <strong class="kw">n log n</strong>. {cite("merge")}'),
('Growth is not a stopwatch.',f'<strong class="kw">O(n²)</strong> and <strong class="kw">O(n log n)</strong> describe how work scales, not seconds. Input, constants and hardware still matter; some methods even change which operations they need. {cite("elementary")}')]),
chapter('models','03','You can beat a limit<br>by changing the question.','EXPOSE THE ASSUMPTIONS',[
('Comparisons buy information.',f'Four distinct items have <strong class="kw">24 possible orders</strong>. A yes/no comparison decision tree needs at least five levels somewhere to distinguish them: four binary answers distinguish at most 16 possibilities. {cite("merge")}'),
('Or use what the keys tell you.',f'With small integer keys, <strong class="kw">counting sort</strong> can directly count occurrences. Its O(n + k) work includes the key range k; this is extra information the comparison-only model does not use. {cite("count")}'),
('One digit at a time.',f'<strong class="kw">Radix sort</strong> can order the units digit, then the tens digit. Each pass must preserve the order of equal digits so the previous pass keeps doing useful work. {cite("radix")}'),
('Ties carry information too.',f'A <strong class="kw">stable</strong> sort preserves the input order of equal keys. If you need a particular tie-breaker in a spreadsheet, specify that second key explicitly instead of assuming undocumented tie behaviour. {cite("contract")}{cite("excel")}'),
('There is more than one resource.',f'A <strong class="kw">sorting network</strong> fixes the comparison schedule; independent pairs can run together. Memory use, parallel depth and total work are different costs, which is why history never converged on one winner. {cite("batcher")}')]),
chapter('surprises','04','The rule can look wrong.<br>The proof can still be right.','DOUBT, TEST, PROVE',[
('“I Can’t Believe It Can Sort”',f'In 2021, Stanley Fung described this all-pairs routine after trying to make incorrect sorts. The <strong class="kw">&lt;</strong> sign looks backwards, yet the result is ascending. {cite("fung")}'),
('It starts by doing something odd.',f'The first outer pass pulls a <strong class="kw">global maximum</strong> to the first position. Watch the whole pass before judging the direction of the result. {cite("fung")}'),
('Find the stronger promise.',f'After outer pass i, the prefix through i is ordered and its last item holds a maximum. At the last pass, that prefix is the entire array; an <strong class="kw">invariant</strong> explains the surprise. {cite("proof")}'),
('AI searched for a better tiny program.',f'<strong class="kw">AlphaDev</strong> used reinforcement learning to discover small sorting routines published in 2023 and incorporated into LLVM libc++. It optimised low-level building blocks, not the asymptotic limit for arbitrary lists. {cite("alpha")}'),
('Keep asking what “better” means.',f'In July 2026, researchers reported <strong class="kw">linear data moves</strong> alongside near-optimal comparisons in an in-place randomised method. The discovery changes a resource tradeoff; it does not make every existing sort obsolete. {cite("moves")}')])]
